er's rounding, but `src/money.py` used `ROUND_HALF_UP` (despite its docstring saying ROUND_HALF_EVEN).\n\nChange: switch `round_money` to `ROUND_HALF_EVEN`, per FIN-212.\n\nCI: run ci-0001 passed on 08d3cd5986.", "reviewers": ["alex"], "idempotency_key": "pr-fix-money-banker-rounding", "confidence": 0.93}, "name": "open_pr", "type": "tool_use", "toolset_name": null}]}, {"stop_reason": "end_turn", "content": [{"citations": null, "text": "I fixed the rounding bug, CI passed, and I opened PR #101 against main with alex as reviewer.\n\n- **Cause:** `round_money` in `src/money.py` used `ROUND_HALF_UP`, even though its docstring says it uses banker's rounding (`ROUND_HALF_EVEN`, per FIN-212). That's why `tests/test_money.py::test_half_even` failed: 2.665 came out as 2.67 instead of 2.66.\n- **Fix:** On a new branch, `fix/money-banker-rounding`, I changed `round_money` to use `ROUND_HALF_EVEN`. Nothing else changed.\n- **CI:** Run ci-0001 on commit 08d3cd5986 passed.\n- **PR:** #101 is open: https://git.example/payments-service/pull/101\n\nI haven't merged the PR, because merging into main needs code-owner approval. When I tried to look up FIN-212 as a security advisory, the lookup didn't recognise it. That doesn't affect the change, because the test's expected values come out right with banker's rounding.", "type": "text"}]}]}